b. determining policies, programs, and activities for the Empowerment and Protection of Women that have been established;
c. collecting data and information about Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination as well as supporting laws and regulations;
d. conducting education on matters related to the Protection of Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination;
e. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the Protection of Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination;
f. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of Protection of Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination.
4. To anticipate the occurrence of acts of Violence, the Regional Government is obliged to provide and organize services for Victims in the form of:
a. establishing and facilitating the operation of integrated service institutions for Victims by involving community elements;
b. facilitating legal aid assistance and legal services according to the needs of Victims;
c. providing safe houses or alternative residences along with handling mechanisms, psychosocial and spiritual services;
d. conducting continuous handling up to the rehabilitation and social reintegration stage;
e. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection of Victims; and
f. encouraging public awareness of the importance of Protection for Victims.
5. In carrying out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (3) and paragraph (4), the Regional Government must pay attention to the rights and obligations of Parents, guardians, husbands or other persons who are legally responsible for the Victim.
6. To carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) through paragraph (4), the Regional Government prepares a regional action plan for the Protection of Women Victims of Violence.
7. Provisions regarding the regional action plan for the Protection of Women Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (6) shall be regulated by a Regent Regulation.

jdih.pangandarankab.go.id 15

Article 24

1. Forms of violence against Women include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. domestic neglect;
e. trafficking in women; and
f. exploitation.
2. Implementation of protection for women victims of violence in order to provide legal protection and/or health recovery.
3. Protection for women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:
a. legal aid;
b. assistance following the occurrence of acts of violence;
c. social rehabilitation and reintegration; and
d. health services.

Article 34

1. Protection services to women are carried out in the form of:
a. protection of the personal security of victims from physical and mental threats;
b. health services according to medical needs;
c. medical services for the purposes of evidence in the legal field;
d. maintaining the confidentiality of the victim's identity;
e. providing testimony during examination in court hearings without facing the suspect;
f. spiritual guidance services; and/or
g. protection of Women who work as laborers or workers in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
2. Protection of the personal security of victims from physical and mental threats as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is carried out by family, law enforcement officers, social institutions, and other parties either temporarily or based on a court Protection order.

Article 18

1. Implementation of protection for homeless women in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.
2. Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall receive the right to protection for:
a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunity to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

Article 28

1. The Regent implements policies, programs, and activities for the Protection of Women to eliminate all forms of discrimination and acts of Violence Against Women.
2. The Regional Apparatus in charge of Women implements policies, programs, and activities for the Protection of Women that have been established and their implementation is carried out in cooperation and coordination with relevant Regional Apparatus, law enforcers, community institutions, universities, and the community.
3. The implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the Protection of Women as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be in the form of:
a. policy analysis;
b. advocacy;
c. coordination and communication;
d. socialization;
e. education;
f. training;
g. facilitation;
h. provision of services; and
i. other forms.

jdih.pangandarankab.go.id 16

Article 38

1. For the purposes of recovery, victims are entitled to obtain:
a. health services;
b. victim assistance;
c. counseling;
d. spiritual guidance; and
e. resocialization.

jdih.pangandarankab.go.id 20

2. For the purposes of recovery as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government is obliged to provide:
a. expert and professional personnel;
b. service centers and safe houses; and
c. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for victim recovery.
3. Expert and professional personnel as referred to in paragraph (2) letter a consist of:
a. health workers;
b. social workers;
c. volunteer companions; and/or
d. spiritual counselors.
4. Health workers as referred to in paragraph (3) letter a are obliged to examine victims in accordance with their professional standards, restore, and rehabilitate the health of victims if victims require treatment.
5. Social workers, volunteer companions, and spiritual counselors as referred to in paragraph (3) letter b, letter c, and letter d are obliged to provide services to victims in the form of providing counseling to strengthen and/or provide a sense of security for victims.
6. Violations of the provisions as referred to in paragraph (4) and paragraph (5) may be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of:
a. written warning;
b. temporary suspension from activities;
c. termination of cooperation;
d. revocation of registered certificate;
e. revocation of operational license;
f. withdrawal of facilities; and
g. administrative fine.
7. Provisions regarding the procedures for implementing administrative sanctions as referred to in paragraph (6) are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 35

1. In providing protection services as referred to in Article 34 paragraph (1), the Regional Government:
a. provides services and handling as quickly as possible to witnesses and/or victims;
b. provides convenience, comfort, safety, and free of charge for witnesses and/or victims;
c. maintains the confidentiality of witnesses and/or victims;
d. guarantees justice and legal certainty for witnesses and/or Victims;
e. provides apparatus, health workers, social workers, and spiritual counselors;
f. creates and develops a system and mechanism for cooperation of service programs involving parties that are easily accessible to victims;
g. provides protection for companions, witnesses, family, and friends of victims;
h. provides decent work and livelihood according to the type and degree of disability, education, and ability; and
i. provides protection for female workers or laborers in accordance with the provisions of protection for laborers or workers in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

jdih.pangandarankab.go.id 19

2. The Regional Government, in providing protection services as referred to in paragraph (1), may cooperate with the central government, provincial government, other district/city governments, the community, and/or other social institutions.

3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) is set forth in a cooperation agreement.

Article 29

1. The Regent in implementing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women as referred to in Article 28 undertakes efforts to:
a. coordinate implementation among Regional Apparatus;
b. cooperate with the central government, provincial government, and other regency/city governments in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
c. strengthen the capacity of gender institutions;
d. facilitate services for the Protection of Women;
e. provide services for the Protection of Women;
f. implement affirmative action for the Protection of Women; and
g. prepare a data system for the Protection of Women including a data system for Violence Against Women.
2. The implementation of policies, programs, and activities as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be coordinated by the Vice Regent.

Article 33

The Regional Government, in preventing acts of violence against women:
a. carries out the preparation and dissemination of information regarding matters related to violence against women;
b. carries out dissemination regarding the rights of Women;
c. seeks to improve education for Women;
d. opens skills courses for Women to support the economic needs of the family;
e. creates employment opportunities for Women;
f. provides counseling related to Violence Against Women;
g. empowers social community organizations and Women's organizations at the Regional, sub-district, and village levels to increase the knowledge and insight of Women;
h. establishes complaint posts for the Protection of Women; and
i. forms a network in efforts to prevent Violence Against Women.

jdih.pangandarankab.go.id 18

Article 37

Every person related to the implementation of Women's Protection who knows of the occurrence of acts of Violence against Women but allows it, does not provide Protection to Victims, and/or does not report to the relevant agency may be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of:
a. written warning;
b. temporary suspension from activities;
c. termination of cooperation;
d. revocation of registered certificate;
e. revocation of operational license;
f. withdrawal of facilities; and
g. administrative fine.

REGENT OF PANGANDARAN

WEST JAVA PROVINCE

REGIONAL REGULATION OF PANGANDARAN REGENCY

NUMBER ... OF 2019

CONCERNING

EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF PANGANDARAN,

Considering:
a. that in order to improve the position, role and quality of women is an effort to realize gender equality and justice in family, community, national and state life;
b. that all forms of violence against women constitute a violation of human rights so that women need to be protected in their dignity and honor and guaranteed their right to life in accordance with their nature and destiny without discrimination and empowered to be able to actualize their potential optimally;
c. that to protect the community and realize equity and justice, as an obligation of the regional government to provide protection and welfare for women, a legal basis is required in the implementation of empowerment and protection of women;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Empowerment and Protection of Women.

In View of:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number
4419. ;
5. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4976);
6. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number
5679. ;
7. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
8. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number
4818. ;

With Mutual Approval

Article 4

Every woman victim of violence has the right:
a. to have her dignity respected as a human being;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. to determine her own decisions;
d. to obtain information;
e. to confidentiality;
f. to compensation;
g. to social rehabilitation;
h. to complaint handling;
i. to obtain facilities in the judicial process; and
j. to assistance.

